# candor-java self-gate (SPEC §7.12): the analyzer reads bytecode and writes reports — Fs/Env
# are its job. It must never reach the network, a database, or IPC — anywhere, ever.
#
# It spawns a subprocess in exactly ONE place: `candor verify`, the dynamic honesty oracle, which
# runs YOUR program under a -javaagent to check candor's static claim against reality. That Exec is
# the feature, not a hidden effect. Prefix scopes can't say "deny Exec everywhere except one
# sub-package", so `ci/self-gate.sh` proves the two halves separately:
#   (1) the WHOLE tool under THIS policy — no Net/Db/Ipc, with no package excluded;
#   (2) the Exec-performing methods are EXACTLY the two declared in that script
#       (`Candor.main`, `verify.VerifyCli.main`) — so a new subprocess anywhere fails the gate,
#       io.poly.candor.verify included.
#
# WHY `deny Net Db Ipc` AND NOT `deny Net Db Exec Ipc`. The stricter string used to live here, and CI
# made it pass by COPYING build/classes and DELETING io/poly/candor/verify first — carving the
# exemption out a whole PACKAGE at a time, so everything in it sat outside the Exec gate and a new
# subprocess there was caught by nothing. The weaker rule over the WHOLE tool, plus a method-level
# declaration, is the stronger gate. A policy is only as strong as the scope it is actually evaluated
# over.
deny Net Db Ipc

# ── THE ARCHITECTURE HALF (§6.2 AS-EFF-009) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# candor ships an architecture gate and, until now, never pointed it at candor. The effects half above
# has been gated since the self-gate landed; the layering half — the capability the JVM case studies are
# about — was exercised only as a QUERY verb in smoke tests. Same omission shape as the code defects this
# project keeps finding: the rule was applied where the work was and never to its sibling.
#
# Every rule below was MEASURED against build/classes before being written (0 violations each), and the
# gate was proved live by inverting one: `forbid io.poly.candor.Candor -> io.poly.candor.model` reports
# 79. A rule nobody has seen fire is not evidence.

# The typed vocabulary is a FOUNDATION: model/ realizes MODEL.md's Effect/EffectSet/Effector/PolicyRule
# and must not reach back into the engine that uses it. Enumerated per class because of a limitation
# noted below, not because these are the only ones that matter.
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Candor
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Query
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Classifier
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Policy
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Interp
forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor.Cha

# `verify` is the DYNAMIC oracle — it runs your program under a -javaagent and checks candor's static
# claim against reality. An oracle that imported the analyzer it audits would be marking its own work.
forbid io.poly.candor.verify -> io.poly.candor.Candor
forbid io.poly.candor.verify -> io.poly.candor.Classifier

# The read surface must not reach into analysis internals: `Query` consumes a REPORT, and a query path
# that could re-derive facts from the analyzer would be a second answer to a question the report already
# answers — the two-copies-drift vein that has produced defects here repeatedly.
forbid io.poly.candor.Query -> io.poly.candor.Interp
forbid io.poly.candor.Query -> io.poly.candor.Cha

# LIMITATION FOUND BY WRITING THIS, and worth more than the rules above. `forbid A -> B` states a
# PROHIBITION; there is no way to state a PERMISSION ("model may depend on nothing but model"). The
# natural spelling `forbid io.poly.candor.model -> io.poly.candor` self-fires — 58 violations — because
# the scope is a prefix and model sits under it. So a leaf package can only be protected by enumerating
# what it must not touch, and that list ROTS: a new engine class is not covered by it, silently. That is
# the allowlist-versus-denylist hazard the classifier has a standing rule about, in the policy language
# itself. Filed in the umbrella backlog.
